From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Craig Johnson <1545411+captainpacket@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 23:28:43 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] ceos: drop service-link env from cEOS pods; cheapen the
 readiness listen check

Measured 2026-09-23 on user-skyforge-demo-estate-13-fu-798b2b0d (275
Services, 63 cEOS on worker-a): during the bringup (03:27-03:29)
and again during the netlab config push (03:33-03:36) EVERY cEOS readiness
probe (0008) timed out at 3s; 52/63 pods show their last Ready transition at
03:35-03:36. NotReady pulls the device's KNE management Service endpoint,
which is the path the analysis system collects over.

The probe body issues no iptables and no Cli, so xtables-lock contention with
the 0017 permit loop is not possible. What it does run is bash, and bash
imports and re-exports its whole environment on start. Kubernetes put a
service-link variable set for every Service port in the namespace into the
container: 4188 vars / 225 KB, 4155 of them SERVICE_*. Same pod, same
node, same minute:

  probe body, pod env      0.27-0.40s wall, 0.22-0.34s USER
  probe body, env -i       0.04-0.065s wall, 0.01-0.02s user
  cEOS, 14-Service lab     0.04-0.05s (same node, env 11 KB)

kubelet's own histogram agrees: the mean successful readiness probe on the 63
demo-estate cEOS was 0.360s, against 0.124s/0.134s for cEOS in the two small
labs on the same node. cEOS requests 25m CPU, so its pod cgroup has
cpu.weight=1. On a saturated node (71/96 cores busy even at rest, an A10 VM
at 32 cores) a probe needing ~0.3s of CPU in a weight-1 cgroup that also
holds ConfigAgent applying a push does not finish in 3s.

1. EnableServiceLinks=false on the cEOS pod. Nothing in cEOS, the seed init
   container or the PostStart script reads a service-link variable, and
   KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST/PORT are still injected. This also cheapens the
   startup probe (wfw), the 0017 permit loop and every exec. The pod drift
   check does not compare it, so rolling the operator restarts no running
   pod; running labs pick it up when their pods are recreated.

2. The LISTEN check reads /proc/net/tcp first and /proc/net/tcp6 only when
   the port is absent there, with awk reading the file directly (no cat).
   Each /proc/net/tcp* read walks the host-wide TCP hash (pod netns share
   it: tcp_child_ehash_entries=0), ~16ms kernel CPU each on this node however
   few sockets the pod has. sshd binds 0.0.0.0:22, so the tcp6 walk is
   normally skipped. Combined, the body drops to 0.021-0.028s (12x).

Probe cadence (period 5, timeout 3, failure 3, success 2) is unchanged:
the fix removes the cost instead of widening the budget.

Tests: TestGetPodDisablesServiceLinks (explicit false, nil is a failure);
TestMgmtReadyScriptExecutes runs the production-rendered script against a
stubbed ip, a logging awk and fixture /proc/net/tcp{,6}. It covers four YES
shapes and six NO shapes, and checks that tcp6 is not read when tcp matches.
Positive controls, each run and seen to fail: dropping EnableServiceLinks,
restoring the cat tcp+tcp6 pipeline, a gate that can only say yes (mgmt
check removed), a wrong port hex, and an unthreaded CIDR list.
---
 controllers/ceoslabdevice_controller.go       |  60 +++-
 .../ceoslabdevice_readiness_cost_test.go      | 260 ++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 305 insertions(+), 15 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 controllers/ceoslabdevice_readiness_cost_test.go

diff --git a/controllers/ceoslabdevice_controller.go b/controllers/ceoslabdevice_controller.go
index 8e0a6df..0e6ea06 100644
--- a/controllers/ceoslabdevice_controller.go
+++ b/controllers/ceoslabdevice_controller.go
@@ -1253,10 +1253,32 @@ func getPod(pod *corev1.Pod, device *ceoslabv1alpha1.CEosLabDevice, secretsAndCo
 			ReadinessProbe: readinessProbe,
 		}},
 		TerminationGracePeriodSeconds: pointer.Int64(0),
-		NodeSelector:                  nodeSelector,
-		Tolerations:                   tolerations,
-		PriorityClassName:             priorityClassName,
-		Volumes:                       volumes,
+		// NO SERVICE-LINK ENV. Kubernetes injects seven <SVC>_SERVICE_*/_PORT_*
+		// variables per Service port for EVERY Service in the namespace, into
+		// every process of the container: PID 1, the PostStart hook and its
+		// permit loop, and every readiness/startup probe exec. A KNE topology
+		// namespace holds one Service per node with ~8 ports, so the env grows
+		// with the lab. Measured 2026-09-23 on a 275-Service DemoEstate lab:
+		// 4188 env vars / 225 KB in the ceos container, 4155 of them service
+		// links. bash (/bin/sh) imports and re-exports that environment on every
+		// start and command, so the 0008 readiness body cost 0.27-0.40s wall,
+		// ~0.3s of it USER CPU, instead of 0.04-0.065s with a clean env (same
+		// pod, same node, same moment). kubelet agreed: the mean successful
+		// readiness probe was 0.36s there against 0.12s for cEOS on the same node
+		// in a 14-Service lab. Under a config push on a saturated node (cEOS
+		// requests 25m, so cgroup cpu.weight=1) that 6-8x CPU demand pushed the
+		// probe past its 3s timeout on 63/63 cEOS, pulling their management
+		// Service endpoints -- the path the analysis system collects over -- for ~4 minutes.
+		// Nothing in cEOS, the seed init container or the lifecycle script reads
+		// a service-link variable (the operator passes everything it needs as
+		// explicit env), and KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST/PORT are still injected.
+		// Not part of the pod drift check: running pods keep their env until
+		// they are recreated, so rolling the operator restarts nothing.
+		EnableServiceLinks: pointer.Bool(false),
+		NodeSelector:       nodeSelector,
+		Tolerations:        tolerations,
+		PriorityClassName:  priorityClassName,
+		Volumes:            volumes,
 	}
 	return nil
 }
@@ -2223,16 +2245,16 @@ func getStartupProbeAPI(device *ceoslabv1alpha1.CEosLabDevice) *corev1.Probe {
 //
 //  1. Management materialized, proven by EITHER of two interface-form signals
 //     (accept either — do not require the first):
-//       a. The default route is the INTERFACE-FORM route EOS installs from
-//          "ip route 0.0.0.0/0 <MgmtIntf>" ("default dev eth0", no `via`) — the
-//          ordinary cEOS whose only 0/0 is the mgmt default, OR
-//       b. a SEEDED RETURN CIDR (0007 installs "ip route <cidr> <MgmtIntf>" for
-//          each SKYFORGE_MGMT_RETURN_CIDRS entry) is present as "<cidr> dev eth0
-//          proto gated" with no `via` — a ROUTED cEOS (e.g. a DC leaf carrying a
-//          data-plane 0/0 into the fabric) whose data default legitimately
-//          displaces the mgmt default from the FIB. proto gated (EOS-installed)
-//          is the anchor: the connected mgmt subnet is proto kernel and the
-//          pre-claim CNI default is via-gateway, so neither false-passes.
+//     a. The default route is the INTERFACE-FORM route EOS installs from
+//     "ip route 0.0.0.0/0 <MgmtIntf>" ("default dev eth0", no `via`) — the
+//     ordinary cEOS whose only 0/0 is the mgmt default, OR
+//     b. a SEEDED RETURN CIDR (0007 installs "ip route <cidr> <MgmtIntf>" for
+//     each SKYFORGE_MGMT_RETURN_CIDRS entry) is present as "<cidr> dev eth0
+//     proto gated" with no `via` — a ROUTED cEOS (e.g. a DC leaf carrying a
+//     data-plane 0/0 into the fabric) whose data default legitimately
+//     displaces the mgmt default from the FIB. proto gated (EOS-installed)
+//     is the anchor: the connected mgmt subnet is proto kernel and the
+//     pre-claim CNI default is via-gateway, so neither false-passes.
 //     This is the phase discriminator: pre-claim (via-form default, no seeded
 //     gated statics) and the post-claim/pre-apply window (no default at all,
 //     no gated statics) both fail BOTH signals; only EOS's own config replay
@@ -2244,6 +2266,13 @@ func getStartupProbeAPI(device *ceoslabv1alpha1.CEosLabDevice) *corev1.Probe {
 //     contract; override via spec.envvars SKYFORGE_MGMT_READY_PORT) has a
 //     LISTEN socket (state 0A in /proc/net/tcp{,6}).
 //
+// The LISTEN check reads /proc/net/tcp first and /proc/net/tcp6 only when the
+// port is not found there. Both files are produced by walking the HOST-WIDE
+// TCP hash tables (net.ipv4.tcp_child_ehash_entries=0: pod netns share them),
+// so each read costs ~16ms of kernel CPU on worker-a no matter how
+// few sockets the pod owns; sshd listens on 0.0.0.0:22, so the tcp6 walk is
+// normally skipped, and awk reads the file itself (no cat process).
+//
 // Deliberately NO `Cli -c` in the probe loop: a FastCli spawn costs 0.5-2s of
 // CPU per exec and can hang while ConfigAgent is busy; at 13 devices probing
 // every 5s forever that is real sustained load, versus ~1-2ms for these kernel
@@ -2266,7 +2295,8 @@ else
 fi
 [ "$mgmt_ok" = 1 ] || exit 1
 ip -4 addr show dev %[1]s | grep -q 'inet .* scope global' || exit 1
-cat /proc/net/tcp /proc/net/tcp6 2>/dev/null | awk '$4=="0A" {split($2,a,":"); if (a[2]=="%[2]s") ok=1} END {exit ok?0:1}'
+listening() { awk '$4=="0A" {split($2,a,":"); if (a[2]=="%[2]s") {ok=1; exit}} END {exit ok?0:1}' "$1" 2>/dev/null; }
+listening /proc/net/tcp || listening /proc/net/tcp6
 `
 
 // getReadinessProbeAPI renders the readiness gate for the ceos container, or
diff --git a/controllers/ceoslabdevice_readiness_cost_test.go b/controllers/ceoslabdevice_readiness_cost_test.go
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..aad7001
--- /dev/null
+++ b/controllers/ceoslabdevice_readiness_cost_test.go
@@ -0,0 +1,260 @@
+package controllers
+
+import (
+	"fmt"
+	"os"
+	"os/exec"
+	"path/filepath"
+	"strings"
+	"testing"
+
+	ceoslabv1alpha1 "github.com/aristanetworks/arista-ceoslab-operator/v2/api/v1alpha1"
+	corev1 "k8s.io/api/core/v1"
+	metav1 "k8s.io/apimachinery/pkg/apis/meta/v1"
+	"sigs.k8s.io/controller-runtime/pkg/client"
+)
+
+// Every cEOS pod must opt out of service-link env injection, whether or not the
+// readiness gate is rendered: the env tax lands on the startup probe (wfw), the
+// PostStart permit loop and every exec, not only on the 0008 gate.
+func TestGetPodDisablesServiceLinks(t *testing.T) {
+	startupCM := map[string]client.Object{
+		startupConfigName("leaf1"): &corev1.ConfigMap{
+			ObjectMeta: metav1.ObjectMeta{Name: startupConfigName("leaf1")},
+			Data:       map[string]string{"startup-config": "hostname leaf1\n"},
+		},
+	}
+	for name, tc := range map[string]struct {
+		spec        ceoslabv1alpha1.CEosLabDeviceSpec
+		cms         map[string]client.Object
+		persistence bool
+	}{
+		"gated mgmt device": {
+			spec:        ceoslabv1alpha1.CEosLabDeviceSpec{IntfMapping: map[string]string{"eth0": "Management0"}},
+			cms:         startupCM,
+			persistence: true,
+		},
+		"plain device, no persistence": {},
+	} {
+		device := &ceoslabv1alpha1.CEosLabDevice{
+			ObjectMeta: metav1.ObjectMeta{Name: "leaf1", Namespace: "demo"},
+			Spec:       tc.spec,
+		}
+		pod := &corev1.Pod{}
+		if err := getPod(pod, device, tc.cms, tc.persistence); err != nil {
+			t.Fatalf("%s: getPod() failed: %v", name, err)
+		}
+		// nil means the API server defaults it to TRUE, so nil is a failure.
+		if pod.Spec.EnableServiceLinks == nil || *pod.Spec.EnableServiceLinks {
+			t.Fatalf("%s: EnableServiceLinks = %v, want explicit false (a 275-Service lab put 4155 vars / 225KB into every probe exec)",
+				name, pod.Spec.EnableServiceLinks)
+		}
+	}
+}
+
+// readyHarness EXECUTES the rendered readiness script (not a string match)
+// against a stubbed ip, a logging awk wrapper and fixture /proc/net/tcp{,6}
+// files, so both halves of the gate are proven: it says YES for a
+// materialized device and NO for every unmaterialized or not-listening shape.
+type readyHarness struct {
+	dir string
+	bin string
+}
+
+const (
+	tcpHeader  = "  sl  local_address rem_address   st tx_queue rx_queue tr tm->when retrnsmt   uid  timeout inode\n"
+	tcp6Header = "  sl  local_address                         remote_address                        st tx_queue rx_queue tr tm->when retrnsmt   uid  timeout inode\n"
+)
+
+func tcpLine(port, state string) string {
+	return fmt.Sprintf("   0: 00000000:%s 00000000:0000 %s 00000000:00000000 00:00000000 00000000     0        0 4242 1 0000000000000000 100 0 0 10 0\n", port, state)
+}
+
+func tcp6Line(port, state string) string {
+	return fmt.Sprintf("   0: 00000000000000000000000000000000:%s 00000000000000000000000000000000:0000 %s 00000000:00000000 00:00000000 00000000     0        0 4243 1 0000000000000000 100 0 0 10 0\n", port, state)
+}
+
+func newReadyHarness(t *testing.T) *readyHarness {
+	t.Helper()
+	if _, err := exec.LookPath("sh"); err != nil {
+		t.Skip("no sh on PATH")
+	}
+	realAwk, err := exec.LookPath("awk")
+	if err != nil {
+		t.Skip("no awk on PATH")
+	}
+	h := &readyHarness{dir: t.TempDir()}
+	h.bin = filepath.Join(h.dir, "bin")
+	if err := os.MkdirAll(h.bin, 0o755); err != nil {
+		t.Fatal(err)
+	}
+	// ip: route show <target> -> route_<target with / and . as _>; addr -> addr.
+	writeStub(t, filepath.Join(h.bin, "ip"), `S="$STUB_STATE"
+if [ "$2" = route ]; then
+  k=$(printf '%s' "$4" | tr '/.' '__')
+  cat "$S/route_$k" 2>/dev/null
+  exit 0
+fi
+if [ "$2" = addr ]; then cat "$S/addr" 2>/dev/null; exit 0; fi
+exit 0
+`)
+	// awk: record which file each invocation read, then run the real awk.
+	writeStub(t, filepath.Join(h.bin, "awk"), fmt.Sprintf(`for a in "$@"; do last="$a"; done
+echo "$last" >>"$STUB_STATE/awk.log"
+exec %s "$@"
+`, realAwk))
+	return h
+}
+
+func (h *readyHarness) write(t *testing.T, name, body string) {
+	t.Helper()
+	if err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(h.dir, name), []byte(body), 0o644); err != nil {
+		t.Fatal(err)
+	}
+}
+
+// run renders the production script and redirects its two fixed /proc paths
+// into the fixture dir. Returns exit==0 and the files awk read.
+func (h *readyHarness) run(t *testing.T, port int, returnCIDRs string) (bool, []string) {
+	t.Helper()
+	_ = os.Remove(filepath.Join(h.dir, "awk.log"))
+	// Render through the production path (port hex, CIDR list threading).
+	device := &ceoslabv1alpha1.CEosLabDevice{
+		ObjectMeta: metav1.ObjectMeta{Name: "leaf1", Namespace: "demo"},
+		Spec: ceoslabv1alpha1.CEosLabDeviceSpec{
+			IntfMapping: map[string]string{"eth0": "Management0"},
+			EnvVar: map[string]string{
+				"SKYFORGE_MGMT_READY_PORT":   fmt.Sprint(port),
+				"SKYFORGE_MGMT_RETURN_CIDRS": returnCIDRs,
+			},
+		},
+	}
+	probe := getReadinessProbeAPI(device, getEnvVarsMap(device), true)
+	if probe == nil || probe.ProbeHandler.Exec == nil || len(probe.ProbeHandler.Exec.Command) != 3 {
+		t.Fatalf("readiness probe not rendered as [sh -c script]: %#v", probe)
+	}
+	script := probe.ProbeHandler.Exec.Command[2]
+	for _, p := range []string{"/proc/net/tcp6", "/proc/net/tcp"} {
+		if !strings.Contains(script, p) {
+			t.Fatalf("readiness script no longer reads %s; update the harness: %s", p, script)
+		}
+	}
+	// tcp6 first so the tcp replacement does not rewrite its prefix.
+	script = strings.ReplaceAll(script, "/proc/net/tcp6", filepath.Join(h.dir, "net_tcp6"))
+	script = strings.ReplaceAll(script, "/proc/net/tcp", filepath.Join(h.dir, "net_tcp"))
+	cmd := exec.Command("sh", "-c", script)
+	cmd.Env = []string{"PATH=" + h.bin + ":" + os.Getenv("PATH"), "STUB_STATE=" + h.dir}
+	err := cmd.Run()
+	if err != nil {
+		if _, ok := err.(*exec.ExitError); !ok {
+			t.Fatalf("readiness script did not run: %v", err)
+		}
+	}
+	var read []string
+	if b, rerr := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(h.dir, "awk.log")); rerr == nil {
+		for _, l := range strings.Split(strings.TrimSpace(string(b)), "\n") {
+			if l != "" {
+				read = append(read, filepath.Base(l))
+			}
+		}
+	}
+	return err == nil, read
+}
+
+func TestMgmtReadyScriptExecutes(t *testing.T) {
+	const (
+		ifDefault  = "default dev eth0 proto gated scope link src 198.18.1.105 \n"
+		viaDefault = "default via 198.18.0.1 dev eth0 \n"
+		dataDflt   = "default via 10.1.0.1 dev eth1 proto gated \n"
+		gatedCIDR  = "100.64.0.0/10 dev eth0 proto gated scope link src 198.18.1.105 \n"
+		kernelCIDR = "198.18.0.0/15 dev eth0 proto kernel scope link src 198.18.1.105 \n"
+		globalAddr = "2: eth0: <UP>\n    inet 198.18.1.105/15 scope global eth0\n"
+		cidrs      = "100.64.0.0/10,198.18.0.0/15" // env form: comma list
+	)
+	type fixture struct {
+		dflt, cidr100, cidr198, addr, tcp, tcp6 string
+	}
+	materialized := fixture{
+		dflt: ifDefault, addr: globalAddr,
+		tcp: tcpHeader + tcpLine("0016", "0A"), tcp6: tcp6Header + tcp6Line("0016", "0A"),
+	}
+	for name, tc := range map[string]struct {
+		f        fixture
+		port     int
+		want     bool
+		wantRead []string // exact awk read set, nil = do not check
+	}{
+		// ---- the gate must be able to say YES ----
+		"materialized, v4 listener: tcp6 is never walked": {
+			f: materialized, port: 22, want: true, wantRead: []string{"net_tcp"},
+		},
+		"listener only in tcp6 (sshd bound [::]:22) falls back": {
+			f: fixture{dflt: ifDefault, addr: globalAddr,
+				tcp: tcpHeader + tcpLine("0016", "01"), tcp6: tcp6Header + tcp6Line("0016", "0A")},
+			port: 22, want: true, wantRead: []string{"net_tcp", "net_tcp6"},
+		},
+		"routed cEOS: data default + seeded gated return CIDR": {
+			f: fixture{dflt: dataDflt, cidr100: gatedCIDR, cidr198: kernelCIDR, addr: globalAddr,
+				tcp: tcpHeader + tcpLine("0016", "0A"), tcp6: tcp6Header},
+			port: 22, want: true,
+		},
+		"port override 6030 listening": {
+			f: fixture{dflt: ifDefault, addr: globalAddr,
+				tcp: tcpHeader + tcpLine("178E", "0A"), tcp6: tcp6Header},
+			port: 6030, want: true,
+		},
+		// ---- the gate must be able to say NO ----
+		"pre-claim: CNI via-form default only": {
+			f: fixture{dflt: viaDefault, cidr198: kernelCIDR, addr: globalAddr,
+				tcp: tcpHeader + tcpLine("0016", "0A"), tcp6: tcp6Header},
+			port: 22, want: false,
+		},
+		"post-claim/pre-apply: only the connected (proto kernel) route": {
+			f: fixture{cidr198: kernelCIDR, addr: globalAddr,
+				tcp: tcpHeader + tcpLine("0016", "0A"), tcp6: tcp6Header},
+			port: 22, want: false,
+		},
+		"no global address on eth0": {
+			f: fixture{dflt: ifDefault, addr: "2: eth0: <UP>\n",
+				tcp: tcpHeader + tcpLine("0016", "0A"), tcp6: tcp6Header},
+			port: 22, want: false,
+		},
+		"port 22 only ESTABLISHED, never LISTEN": {
+			f: fixture{dflt: ifDefault, addr: globalAddr,
+				tcp: tcpHeader + tcpLine("0016", "01"), tcp6: tcp6Header + tcp6Line("0016", "06")},
+			port: 22, want: false,
+		},
+		"listening, but on another port": {
+			f: fixture{dflt: ifDefault, addr: globalAddr,
+				tcp: tcpHeader + tcpLine("0017", "0A"), tcp6: tcp6Header + tcp6Line("0050", "0A")},
+			port: 22, want: false,
+		},
+		"proc files unreadable": {
+			f:    fixture{dflt: ifDefault, addr: globalAddr},
+			port: 22, want: false,
+		},
+	} {
+		h := newReadyHarness(t)
+		for file, body := range map[string]string{
+			"route_default":       tc.f.dflt,
+			"route_100_64_0_0_10": tc.f.cidr100,
+			"route_198_18_0_0_15": tc.f.cidr198,
+			"addr":                tc.f.addr,
+		} {
+			h.write(t, file, body)
+		}
+		if tc.f.tcp != "" {
+			h.write(t, "net_tcp", tc.f.tcp)
+		}
+		if tc.f.tcp6 != "" {
+			h.write(t, "net_tcp6", tc.f.tcp6)
+		}
+		got, read := h.run(t, tc.port, cidrs)
+		if got != tc.want {
+			t.Fatalf("%s: readiness = %v, want %v (awk read %v)", name, got, tc.want, read)
+		}
+		if tc.wantRead != nil && strings.Join(read, ",") != strings.Join(tc.wantRead, ",") {
+			t.Fatalf("%s: awk read %v, want exactly %v (each /proc/net/tcp* read walks the host-wide TCP hash)", name, read, tc.wantRead)
+		}
+	}
+}
-- 
2.55.0

